WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.302
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-winning

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.005
actress you know
from films such as "The Piano,"

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:07.673
"The Incredibles,"
and "The Big Sick."

00:00:07.673 --> 00:00:09.075
She stars in "Mr. Mayor,"

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.511
which returns tomorrow night
here on NBC.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.712
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.313
-Is there something you need
from me, Arpi?

00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:17.449
-I need you to explain to me
why you think our administration

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:20.486
needs outside help
from a bunch of boss babies.

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.322
-Because if there's anything
I've learned

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:23.722
after a year in government,

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:26.158
it's that we should do
everything differently.

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:29.228
-Hey, man, I'm your most
progressive progressive.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:31.730
America Ferrera told me
to settle down

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.299
at the Women's March.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.001
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.704
our friend Holly Hunter,
everyone!

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.372
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:45.978
♪♪

00:00:45.978 --> 00:00:52.585
♪♪

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.687
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:58.023
Hello! It is so lovely
to have you back here.

00:00:58.023 --> 00:00:59.225
-Thanks.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.926
-You know, you were
a long-time New Yorker.

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:03.062
Does it make you happy
to be back in the city?

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:07.333
-Oh, well, yeah. I mean,
I love doing "Mr. Mayor" in LA

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.836
because LA
is so set up for work.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.237
-Yes.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:13.206
-I mean, if you need something
yesterday, they can do it.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:14.474
-Yeah.

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:17.943
-But it's always a pleasure
to come home.

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:22.347
You know, I've been here
since 1980, and it's my home.

00:01:22.347 --> 00:01:23.883
-What was -- So 1980.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.719
You were a young actor
in the business.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.688
I'm assuming
your first apartments here

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.956
were not super nice?

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:32.191
-No, I mean, really,
it was like a --

00:01:32.191 --> 00:01:34.193
It was like
me or the cockroaches.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:36.963
-Yeah.
I think in 1980,

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.399
cockroaches were legally allowed
to vote in the city.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.636
-Yeah, I mean,
like, when the light --

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:46.139
When you turn the lights out,
they came out.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:47.440
-Yeah.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.908
-You know, it was their time.
-It was their time.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.543
I always --
Did you ever find this?

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:52.645
My first apartment in New York
had a cockroach problem,

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.313
and I always thought
the interesting thing

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:56.916
was when one died,
it would, like, walk

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.017
to the middle of the kitchen
and then flip over there.

00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:00.653
Like, as a way of saying,

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:04.057
"I just want you to know
I was here the whole time.

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:06.226
My entire life was spent
in your walls."

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:08.360
-Yes, and no one comes out
and takes them.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.396
You would think they would carry
their dead, but they don't.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.832
-No. It's nothing.
Well, maybe they do.

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:16.636
Maybe they drag them out,
and then they all scutter back.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:19.705
-I mean, like, they lived
in my silverware drawer.

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:22.241
I mean, that's where, you know,
in the middle of the night,

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:23.877
it was like -- Yeah.
-No, I mean,

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.912
you were doing pretty well
if you had a silverware drawer.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.280
-[ Laughter ]
-They didn't live in mine

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.516
'cause it was just
white plastic wrapped in --

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:31.918
It was all the take-out.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.186
-Yeah, the take-out stuff.
-Yeah.

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:35.721
You also lived in Pittsburgh.

00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:37.290
You went to Carnegie Mellon,
which is also where your costar

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.158
Ted Danson went to school,
although different times.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.492
Let's be honest.
Ted is, I mean...

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:41.827
-Oh, my God.
-...so much older than you.

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:43.763
-He's like a dinosaur.

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.998
-I mean, we could talk about him
because he's not here,

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:47.700
but, I mean,
he's just a very old man.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.403
-Yeah. Whew!
-But you --

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.804
So this is interesting.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.840
You went back.
It's a wonderful theater school.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.376
You went back and you taught
a class recently.

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:59.578
-Well, you know, I thought
it was going to be really fun...

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:01.814
-Yeah.
-...and kind of stress-free

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.349
to go talk to the students

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.152
and, like, moderate
their acting classes

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.753
and maybe give them
some direction.

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:11.524
And the person who was taking me
around said,

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.292
"Oh, I hope you don't mind.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:17.863
Judd Apatow is going to be
in the acting class today."

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:21.133
I was like, "What?!"

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:24.670
And his daughter was thinking
about going to Carnegie.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.240
-Yeah.
-So he was in the class

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.474
the entire time that I was,

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.244
like, "directing,"
and it was so --

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:34.781
I mean, I knew I was going to be
found out as a complete fraud.

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:36.248
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:38.351
But, anyway,
he was absolutely lovely.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:40.853
And then after that,
I ended up working with him

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.221
on "The Big Sick."

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:44.757
So I didn't do terribly.
-You didn't do terribly.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.326
You basically --
You were secretly auditioning.

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:47.527
You know who's mad,
is all the kids

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:49.062
that you were teaching them,
and they're like,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.597
"And she's the one
who got the part!"

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:53.365
She was supposed to
be teaching us!"

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:54.567
And then Apatow was like,

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:55.868
"Oh, who's this teacher
up there?"

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.304
-Yes. Yes.
-Do you think you're a good --

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.239
Did you feel like --
I mean, obviously,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:02.775
you probably had great
acting teachers coming up.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.877
Did you feel like
you could impart wisdom

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:06.245
as an acting teacher yourself?

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:07.680
-no.
-[ Laughter ]

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.448
-Yeah. I think I've had
great teachers,

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:11.350
but I don't know
if I'd be a great teacher.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.551
I think it's two
different things.

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:13.953
-No, it's a completely
different thing.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:17.556
I mean, I just wanted to be kind
to those kids because...

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:18.691
-Right.

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.626
-You know, I can't
really impart wisdom.

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:23.062
-Yeah.
-I can't tell people what to do.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.431
You know, whenever
you tell somebody, like,

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.733
the best way to do it,
they do it the opposite way,

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:30.035
and they're, you know,
incredibly successful.

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:32.505
So I don't.
-The other thing is, certainly,

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.674
in a small period of time
that you were with each student,

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:36.142
like, you probably
can't tell enough

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.878
about what kind of actor
each kid is

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.880
to, like, give them -- Yeah.
-Totally not.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.715
-The best path forward, yeah.
-Yeah, I mean --

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:44.984
No, I would never
be that presumptuous.

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.219
-Yeah.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:49.188
You work with
an incredible ensemble cast.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:51.524
"Mr. Mayor" has Tina Fey,
Robert Carlock.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:53.192
I'm lucky enough to call
both of them friends.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.894
They're both, like, the funniest
people in the world,

00:04:55.894 --> 00:04:58.263
but they're also, like,
brilliant scientists.

00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:00.700
The way they build comedy is --

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.002
has an incredible amount
of patience and craft to it.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.537
-Such a great way to put it.

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.105
They are like scientists.
-Yeah.

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:08.441
-You know, because they're
so interested in character.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:11.978
It's not -- They're so funny,
but they're not just --

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:16.782
They're so character-driven
writers.

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:20.753
So, once again, you know,
they're so particular

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:24.056
with each one
of the cast members.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:27.360
I mean, Tina's direction
is just brilliant.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.528
-Yeah. As an actor, it must be
so great to be with somebody

00:05:29.528 --> 00:05:31.063
who knows
exactly what they want.

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:33.765
Like, that they can ask for it
and you can deliver it.

00:05:33.765 --> 00:05:37.970
-Yeah, you can just feel free
really to jump.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.005
You can just jump off the cliff,

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:42.641
and, you know,
you're going to be caught.

00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:44.710
-You mentioned Tina
being wonderful as a director.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.579
You worked with probably

00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:48.247
one of the finest
female directors we know,

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.850
Jane Campion, in "The Piano"
and now "Power of the Dog."

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.119
She's nominated for an Oscar.
It's really exciting.

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:56.122
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:58.724
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:03.162
I mean, that is -- That's --
1994, you do "The Piano,"

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:04.997
and what was that experience
like to work with someone --

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:06.866
Did you know Jane at all
before you did that film?

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.502
-No, no, but I just adore her
and love her,

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:11.036
and this is just
the greatest thing.

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:13.973
I could almost, you know,
burst out crying right now,

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:16.575
I'm so --
I'm so thrilled for her.

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:19.178
-Yeah.
-You know, she's an artist.

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:21.614
She is an artist
of the first degree.

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:24.884
She's, you know,
a completely silly person.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:28.754
She's totally absurdly funny.

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.422
-I mean, so, it's interesting
because, you know,

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:31.991
I talked
to Benedict Cumberbatch,

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:34.126
who obviously is wonderful in
the film and nominated, as well,

00:06:34.126 --> 00:06:36.028
and he was saying, you know,
when he went into this,

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:37.797
he was a little intimidated
because, of course,

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.231
she was, at that point,
Jane Campion.

00:06:39.231 --> 00:06:41.134
Did you feel the same thing
when you did "The Piano"?

00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:42.501
At what point in her career
was it?

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:44.370
Or did it feel --
Did you know --

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.071
Were you like,
"Oh, my God, it's Jane Campion"?

00:06:46.071 --> 00:06:48.407
-"An Angel at My Table"
I thought was extraordinary.

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:50.242
"Sweetie" was amazing.

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:54.747
So, yeah, Jane came with
a certain calling card.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:57.250
There was a pedigree there
that I knew about.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.750
-Yeah.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.219
So, having kids that I'm slowly
introducing animated films to,

00:07:01.219 --> 00:07:03.222
do kids -- especially
with "The Incredibles,"

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:04.790
do they ever hear your voice

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.792
and know that you are
one of The Incredibles?

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.261
-Yes.
-Yeah. Is that exciting?

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:11.263
-Yeah, it's actually kind of --
it's kind of heartbreaking.

00:07:11.263 --> 00:07:12.565
I love it.

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:14.667
-Do they all --
Are they all happy about it?

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:16.135
Because my kids --
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:17.637
-Okay. That's good.
-Yeah.

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:20.272
But you know what's so weird
about "The Incredibles"?

00:07:20.272 --> 00:07:22.941
There were 13 years between the
first one and the second one.

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:29.549
Like, I get people who are,
like, 20 who are rabid fans,

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:31.183
and I'm not really used to that.

00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:33.152
-Yeah.
-So that's cool

00:07:33.152 --> 00:07:36.221
because they caught
"Incredibles" part two,

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:38.123
and they love it, as well.

00:07:38.123 --> 00:07:41.593
So it's such a cool
swinging door.

00:07:41.593 --> 00:07:43.029
-Yeah, it's a real --
Anything --

00:07:43.029 --> 00:07:45.698
Look, anything that expands
the fan base, right?

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:47.366
-True.
-Look, it's a business, Holly.

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:48.935
Let's be honest.

00:07:48.935 --> 00:07:50.870
Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.105
It's always such a delight
to talk to you.

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.540
-My Pleasure.
-Holly Hunter.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.275
The second season of "Mr. Mayor"

00:07:56.275 --> 00:07:58.578
premieres tomorrow night
here on NBC.

